## ABSTRACT
It was known that mesoporous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with hierarchical pores and unsaturated metal sites can effectively inhibit the shuttle effect of lithium polysulfides in lithium-sulfur battery, however, the unsatisfactory structural stability and electrical conductivity limit the application of mesoporous MOFs (MMOFs) in Li-S batteries. Aiming at sensible solutions, the conductive polyaniline (PANI) was incorporated into the MMOF to enhance the discharge capacity and the cycling stability of proposed Li-S batteries, as the stability and the conductivity of the MMOF cathode was improved simultaneously. The activated MMOF-PANI provides physical and chemical adsorption of polysulfides against their shuttle effect. Moreover, the introduction of PANI into the channels of MMOF effectively improves the conductivity of MMOF, thus improving the electrochemical performance of the MMOF-PANI-based batteries. Benefiting from these synergetic effects, the S@MMOF-PANI cathode delivers improved electrochemical performance including excellent rate performance and cycling stability. The battery shows an initial capacity of \(777.7\;\;\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\) at \(2.0\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and a low decay rate of \(0.06\%\) per cycle in 1,000 cycles and approximately a repeatable rate performance.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic framework, conductivity, mesoporous, shuttle effect, Li-S battery
## 1 Introduction
With the increase in global demand, the design of advanced materials for energy storage systems has attracted considerable attention from researchers worldwide [1, 2]. Taking advantages of high theoretical capacity, low cost, and environmental benignity, lithium-sulfur batteries (LiSBs) have been one of the most advanced energy storage systems [3, 4]. Moreover, elemental sulfur, when employed as cathode material in LiSBs, has a high theoretical specific capacity of \(1,675\;\mathrm{mAh\cdot g^{-1}}\) and a specific energy of \(2,600\;\mathrm{Wh\cdot kg^{-1}}\) [5]. Hence, it is one of the most promising cathode materials for next-generation energy storage systems [6]. However, several key issues that exist in LiSBs hinder their commercial application, namely, quick capacity fading, low active-material utilization, and poor cathode conductivity [7]. Most of these problems are mainly caused by the inherent insulation property of sulfur and the shuttling effect of high-order lithium polysulfides (LiPSs) intermediates. Exploring appropriate materials that can host sulfur reliably and efficiently is the key to high-performance LiSBs electrodes.At the right moment, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed by metal/metal cluster nodes and organic linkers are considered as ideal hosts for loading sulfur and intermediate products (dissolved LiPSs) during electrochemical reactions [8, 9]. It is reported that factors like unsaturated metal sites, suitable porous structure and functionalized organic linkers are advantageous to inhibit the shuttle effect of LiPSs, achieving excellent battery performance [10]. On the one hand, the unsaturated metal sites were utilized as Lewis acids for the physical/chemical adsorption of polysulfides [11–13]. And the metal sites with special electronic structures can catalyze the redox conversion of LiPSs. On the other hand, the functionalized organic linkers with N, O, S, and aromaticity et al. active sites can anchor LiPSs through physical 