At the right moment, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed by metal/metal cluster nodes and organic linkers are considered as ideal hosts for loading sulfur and intermediate products (dissolved LiPSs) during electrochemical reactions [8, 9]. It is reported that factors like unsaturated metal sites, suitable
porous  structure  and  functionalized  organic  linkers  are advantageous to inhibit the shuttle effect of LiPSs, achieving excellent  battery  performance  [10].  On  the  one  hand,  the unsaturated metal sites were utilized as Lewis acids for the physical/chemical adsorption of polysulfides [11–13]. And the metal sites with special electronic structures can catalyze the redox conversion of LiPSs. On the other hand, the functionalized organic linkers with N, O, S, and aromaticity et al. active sites can anchor LiPSs through physical adsorption [14–16]. Furthermore, transition metal ions were anchored in organic ligands to adsorb LiPSs and promote their redox transformation [17]. Additionally, the particle shape and size of MOFs allow modulation of the adsorption ability for LiPSs owing to the diverse surface properties of different crystal planes [18]. Besides, the pores of MOFs were utilized as the platform for anchoring LiPSs and the micro-reactor for  polysulfides  transformation  due  to  their  well-developed porosity and high surface area [19, 20].
Due to its high porosity and large specific surface area, mesoporous MOFs (MMOFs) are considered as the ideal candidates for the cathode host of Li-S batteries. Nevertheless, the unsatisfactory structural stability resulting from the fragile large pores limits the application of MMOFs in Li-S batteries. It is a hander challenge to improve the structural stability of cathode MMOFs  to  meet  the  demands  of  high  electrochemical performance of Li-S batteries [21]. It was reported that the
polydopamine was introduced into the MMOF to support its skeleton [22]. Ameliorating the existing method, the conductive polyaniline (PANI) was incorporated into the large channel of the MMOF to support and stabilize the whole porous network and the porosity of MMOF was maintained.
Additionally, the unsatisfactory electrical conductivity limits the efficiency of MOFs as the ideal cathode host. Aiming at the conductivity improvement, one strategy would be to utilize MOFs as the precursors to obtain porous carbon materials through carbonification [23–26]. Carbon materials with different morphologies and porous properties were obtained to encapsulate sulfur and lithium polysulfides. Another strategy is to combine MOFs with conductive agents [27]. Graphene [28–30], carbon nanotubes (CNTs) [31–33], and polymers [34] with good electrical conductivity were utilized as the conductive agents to improve the electrical conductivity of MOFs. The conductive additives, which are usually coated on the surface or interspersed in the bulk phase of MOFs particles, can effectively regulate the electrical conductivity and improve the electrochemical performance of MOFs-based batteries, but the intrinsic conductivity of MOFs materials is rarely addressed. Introducing conductive PANI into the channels is a novel efficient strategy to greatly enhance the overall conductivity of MOF substrates through permeable infiltration of conductive polymers in the mesoporous networks.
Inspired by the above, in this work, the conducting PANI was encapsulated into the channels of a MMOF to enhance its structural stability and whole conductivity simultaneously. Meanwhile, the introduction of conductive polymers into the channels can promote the electron transport between the skeleton and the active substances adsorbed in the channels during charge-discharge processes. The composite material with hierarchical pores was utilized as the efficient cathode host of sulfur in LiSBs. All these advantages of MMOF-PANI have contributed to the enhanced electrochemical performance of target LiSBs, including excellent rate performance and superior cycling stability. The cathode showed good electrochemical stability during the charge/discharge cycles, demonstrating an effective way to achieve the potential practical application of LiSBs.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Preparation of MMOF, MMOF-PANI, and S@MMOF-PANI
Synthesis of ligand \(\mathbf{N},\mathbf{N}^{2}\) -bis(3-carboxy-4-hydroxyphenyl)- 1,4,5,8-naphthalenetetradicarboximide \(\mathbf{(H_{4}N D I S A)}\) [35]. \(1.99\ \mathrm{g}\) naphthalene-1,4,5,8-tetracarboxylic dianhydride \((7.44\mathrm{~mmol})\) and \(2.34\ \mathrm{~g~}\) 5-amino-2-hydroxybenzoic acid \((15.3\ \mathrm{\mmol})\) were dissolved in \(70~\mathrm{mL}\) anhydrous dimethylformamide (DMF) under nitrogen, and the mixture was stirred vigorously at \(130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . After cooling to room temperature, the raw product was filtered out and washed with DMF, diethyl ether and methanol, successively, and then dried under vacuum at \(60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to yield an orange powder. And then, the powder was soaked in \(2~\mathrm{M}\) HCl and stirred for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) for purification.
Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Ni}_{2}(\mathbf{NDISA})\) (MMOF) [34]. 0.29 g \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \(\left(1.0\,\mathrm{\mmol}\right)\) and \(0.29\,\mathrm{~g~}\) \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{NDISA}\) \((0.5\,\mathrm{\mmol})\) were mixed in glass vial with a solvent mixture \(\mathrm{(DMF/CH_{3}O H=}\) \(20/5\;\mathrm{mL})\) and the mixture was ultrasonicated for \(15\;\mathrm{min}\) . The vial was placed in a Teflon-lined stainless-steel reactor and then seated in the oven at \(130~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) . After cooling, the product was filtered out and washed with DMF and methanol thrice, respectively. And then the product was immersed in methanol for three days and the methanol solvent was changed every day. The final product was stored in methanol for later use.
Synthesis of MMOF-PANI. \(100\,\mathrm{~mg~}\) PANI was soaked in \(100~\mathrm{mL}\) DMF and the turbid liquid was ultrasound for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) . After precipitating for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) , the dark green supernatant was used as the solvent in \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{NDISA})\) synthesis to obtain MMOF-PANI. Finally, the product was centrifuged and washed with methanol thrice.
Synthesis of S@MMOF-PANI. The original MMOF-PANI was heated at \(155~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in vacuum for \(12~\mathrm{h}\) , after cooling to room temperature, the complex was then mixed with sulfur in a ratio of 3:7 followed by grinding to obtain a uniform mixture. The mixture was encapsulated in stainless-steel reaction kettle lined with polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) at \(155~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . After cooling naturally to room temperature, the S@MMOF-PANI was obtained.
## 2.2 Adsorption tests of lithium polysulfides
The \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) solution was obtained by the comproportionation of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) and sublimed sulfur with a molar ratio of 1:7 in 1,3-dioxolane (DOL)/1,2-dimethoxyethane (DME) (volume of 1:1). The suspension of raw materials was thoroughly stirred for \(24~\mathrm{{h}}\) to obtained a claybank solution (the concentration of \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) was \(\sim5~\mathrm{mM})\) . Subsequently, \(20~\mathrm{mg}\) MMOF-PANI and \(20~\mathrm{mg}\) CNTs were separately added to the two sub-packed claybank solutions. The mixtures were intensely stirred for \(2\,\,\mathrm{h}\) and then stood to precipitate the electrode materials.
## 2.3 Cell construction and electrochemical measurements
The as-prepared S@MMOF-PANI was blended with CNTs and poly(vinylidene fluoride) (PVDF) binder in a ratio of 7:2:1 in N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP, \(99.5\%\) ) and the mixture was grounded by ball-milling to form a slurry. The slurry was then pasted onto carbon paper and dried in a vacuum oven at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathrm{~h~}\) to obtain a uniformly spread S@MMOF-PANI integrated electrodes. The sulfur mass loading of each S@MMOF-PANI electrodes was \(\sim\,1.5\,\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}c m^{-2}}\) . Other electrodes were prepared using the same procedures. The cathode was separated with lithium anode by Celgard 2400 separator. The electrolyte was DOL and DME (1:1) solvent with lithium bis(trifluoromethylsulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI) (1 M) and \(1\%\) \(\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}\) solutes.
A LAND galvanostatic charge–discharge instrument (the battery testing device of Wuhan Land Electronic Co.,Ltd.) was used to perform electrochemical measurements. The C-rate used in this manuscript is based on the theoretical specific capacity of sulfur \((1\;\;\mathrm{C}\;=\;1,675\;\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}})\) , and the current rate set for tests was referred to the mass of sulfur in the cathode. The charge–discharge voltage range was set to 1.7–2.8 V. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was measured on a CHI660E instrument at the open-circuit potential and the frequency range of the EIS measurements was \(100\;\mathrm{kHz}\) to \(0.1\ \mathrm{Hz}\) with an alternating current (AC) voltage amplitude of \(50\,\mathrm{\mV}\) . cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were executed on a CHI660E instrument with the sweep rates of 0.1 to \(1.0\,\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) .
## 3 Results and discussion
The synthetic strategy of the cathode composite material was shown in Scheme 1 (details are given in the experimental sections). In the presence of PANI in DMF, the \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{NDISA}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) were used as the raw materials to synthesize mesoporous \(\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{NDISA})\) , which in situ encapsulates conducting PANI (MMOF-PANI) as supporting scaffolds to hold up the mesoporous framework. The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) pattern of MMOF-PANI well reproduces that of the as
guest molecules, while the skeleton of original MMOF was collapsed. The introduction of PANI stabilizes the framework without blocking the pore passage and the MOF retains most of its pore structure and high specific surface area. The multistage pore structure produced by the introduction of polymer is beneficial to alleviate the volume expansion during discharging process. The introduction of sulfur causes the occupation of the channel cavities, showing the marked reduction of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption and the disappearance of the pore size distribution at 0.78 and \(1.86\,\mathrm{nm}\) in MMOF-PANI after the sulfur loading (Fig. S2 in the ESM). Unsatisfactorily, the MMOF-PANI surface is coated with residual sulfur layer observed from the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images (Figs. S3 and S4 in the ESM). The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere showed that the MMOF-PANI can be thermally stable up to \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. S5 in the ESM), and it gradually disintegrates as the temperature continues to rise. Additionally, the content of PANI in MMOF-PANI was calculated to be \(12.67\%\) from the TGA under \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) atmosphere (Fig. S6 in the ESM). For S@MMOF-PANI, the sulfur loading was calculated to be \(59.1\%\) (Fig. S5 in the ESM), and the sulfur element is evenly distributed in the complex conformed by the elemental mapping images (Fig. 1(d)).
To estimate the performance of the designed cathode host material, the control sample S@CNTs was synthesized and electrochemically characterized. The conductive PANI improves the conductivity and the structural stability of MMOF, simultaneously. The redox behavior of batteries with S@MMOF-PANI and S@CNTs cathodes was examined with CV at a scan rate of \(0.1\,\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The reduction potentials of S@MMOF-PANI were 2.317 and \(2.037\,\mathrm{V}\), corresponding to the reduction processes from sulfur to soluble long-chain lithium polysulfides and further to insoluble \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), respectively. The oxidation potentials were 2.318 and 2.391 V, corresponding to the oxidation processes from discharging product to soluble long-chain lithium polysulfides and then to sulfur, respectively. For the reductive peaks, the onset potential of the battery with S@MMOF-PANI cathode is higher than that of S@CNTs cathode, indicating the higher kinetics in the conversion reaction of LiPsS promoted by the MMOF-PANI host and the effective inhibition on the shuttle effect of LiPsS [13, 37]. In the CV profiles at different scanning rates, the S@MMOF-PANI shows two pairs of obvious redox peaks (Fig. 2(a)). It means that the S@MMOF-PANI cathode can perform high reaction kinetics in various oxidation and reduction reaction stages even at high scanning rates. For S@CNTs, as the scanning rate increases, the reduction peak in the second stage gradually widens and the oxidation peak in the first stage gradually weakens and finally coincides with the oxidation peak in the second stage (Fig. 2(b)). It indicates that the redox reaction of S@CNTs at each stage cannot be carried out thoroughly and the active substances that have not been utilized gradually increase at high scanning rates. The transference number of lithium ions \((D_{\mathrm{Li}})\) can be calculated from the linear relationship between the peak currents and the square root of sweep speeds (Figs. 2(c) and 2(d), and Table S1 in the ESM) [38, 39]. The \(D_{\mathrm{Li}}\) of S@MMOF-PANI cathode at redox peaks (A, B, C, and D) were calculated to be \(4.58\times10^{-7}\), \(3.49\times10^{-7}\), \(9.37\times10^{-8}\), and \(9.11\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\), which are higher than that of S@CNTs \((3.51\times10^{-7}\), \(2.24\times10^{-7}\), \(2.77\times10^{-8}\), and \(4.23\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}\)). The S@MMOF-PANI cathode showed better \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\)conductivity than S@CNTs. The overpotentials of the two pairs of redox peaks are calculated from the CV curves at different scanning rates (Figs. 2(e) and 2(f)). It can be observed that the redox overpotentials of S@MMOF-PANI are obviously smaller than that of S@CNTs, indicating that the MMOF-PANI can promote the kinetics of redox conversion of lithium polysulfides. Furthermore, the charge
transfer process of electrode materials was further explored by the AC impedance measurements (Fig. S8 in the ESM). As the cathodes were assembled into the batteries, the S@MMOF-PANI showed an initial impedance of \( 5.3\;\Omega \), which was much lower than that of S@CNTs \( (20.6\ \Omega) \), indicating that the S@MMOF-PANI composite presents good charge transfer characteristics based on good conductivity. After 100 cycles of charge–discharge activation, the comparison shows that the battery with S@MMOF-PANI cathode exhibited a relatively lower impedance value \( (7.7\;\Omega) \) than that of S@CNTs \( (8.0\;\Omega) \). Additionally, the AC impedance of the battery with PANI-modified cathode is significantly smaller than that of S@MMOF containing original MMOF before and after charge–discharge cycles (Fig. S9 in the ESM). The introduction of PANI into the channels of MMOF effectively improves the conductivity of MMOF, thus improving the charge transformation of the MMOF-PANI-based batteries and making the redox conversion of polysulfides easier.
Galvanostatic charge/discharge measurements were performed to investigate the electrochemical behavior of the S@MMOF-PANI composite over the voltage range of \( 1.7{-}2.8\,\mathrm{~V~} \). The rate tolerance performance of batteries with different cathodes was explored by galvanostatic charge/discharge at different rates (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)). Compared to S@CNTs cathode, the battery with S@MMOF-PANI cathode delivered a better rate capability, scilicet, the S@MMOF-PANI cathode exhibited good circularity at different rates (from 0.1 to \( 5~\mathrm{C} \) and then back to \( 0.5\;\mathrm{C} \)). It was demonstrated that the S@MMOF-PANI cathode exhibited a tapering capacity from \( 1.179.5\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) at \( 0.1~\mathrm{C} \) to 951.2, 828.8, 727.4, 634.7, 567.2, 499.7 and \( 386.1\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) at 0.2, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, and \( 5.0\;\mathrm{C}, \) and a reversible capacity of \( 807.6\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) with a restoration ratio of \( 97.4\% \) was obtained when the current was decreased from 5.0 to \( 0.5\;\mathrm{C}, \) indicating a highly reversible and low fading capacity of S@MMOF-PANI. By contrast, the S@CNTs cathode retained only 203.0 and \( 173.8\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) with the current rising to 4.0 and \( 5.0\,\textrm{C} \). The battery with S@MMOF-PANI or S@CNTs cathode shows typical charge/discharge curves at
different current densities (Figs. S10 and S11 in the ESM) and the overpotentials of the curves were shown in Fig. 3(c). The voltage plateau at about \( 2.3~\mathrm{V} \) is corresponded to the conversion from S8 molecules to soluble polysulfides \( (\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8},\,\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}, \) or \( \mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}) \), while the plateau at roughly \( 2.1\;\mathrm{V} \) corresponds to the further dissociation of high-order polysulfides into insoluble lithium sulfides \( (\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}/\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}) \) [40]. It can be clearly observed that the overpotentials of the battery with S@MMOF-PANI cathode are obviously lower than that of S@CNTs at the same current density, indicating the higher conversion dynamics of polysulfides.
The Galvanostatic charge–discharge profiles of the S@MMOF-PANI cathode at \( 0.5\;\mathrm{C} \) with a break of \( 24~\mathrm{{h}} \) were carried out (Fig. 3(d)). After standing for \( 24~\mathrm{{h}} \) following the charge process in the \( 100^{\mathrm{th}} \) cycle, the battery showed a discharge capacity of \( 852.5\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \), with a conservation of \( 97.6\% \), indicating that the S@MMOF-PANI cathode shows almost no self-discharging, which is further proved by the time-dependent open circuit voltage test (Fig. S12 in the ESM). For long-term cycling performance, the charge/discharge measurements of batteries with S@MMOF-PANI or S@CNTs at 0.2 to \( 2.0~\mathrm{C} \) between 1.7 and \( 2.8\,\mathrm{~V~} \) were depicted. The S@MMOF-PANI cathode can deliver remarkable cyclic stability compared with the S@CNTs at each current density. The S@MMOF-PANI cathode showed an initial capacity of \( 1,180.8\ \mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) (the highest capacity in the \( 2^{\mathrm{nd}} \) cycle) and retain a capacity of \( 728.5\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) after 200 cycles at \( 0.2\;\mathrm{C} \). By contrast, the S@CNTs cathode exhibited an initial capacity of \( 975.0\;\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) and an ultimate capacity of \( 384.2\;\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \). The average capacity decay rates of the batteries with S@MMOF-PANI cathode are \( 0.19\% \), \( 0.11\% \), and \( 0.06\% \) per cycle at 0.2, 0.5, and 1.0 C (Figs. S13–S15 in the ESM), respectively, which are significantly lower than that of S@CNTs \( (0.30\% \), \( 0.14\% \), and \( 0.12\% \), respectively). In addition, the S@MMOF-PANI cathode delivered good long-term cycling performance with an ultimate capacity of \( 300.0\;\mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) after 1,000 cycles even at the current of \( 2.0\,\textrm{C}, \) accompanied by a decay rate of \( 0.06\% \) per cycle (Fig. 3(e)). By contrast, the S@MMOF cathode exhibits specific capacity of 387 and \( 120\;\mathrm{mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}} \) and poor cycling stability (Figs. S16 and S17 in
the ESM). Even under the high sulfur loading of \(2.5{-}2.6~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), the battery showed initial specific capacity of 984 and \(837\;\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) under the current density of 0.5 and \(1.0~\mathrm{C}\) with good cycling stability (Fig. S18 in the ESM). The electrochemical tests of batteries with different cathodes indicated that the soluble polysulfides were confined into the channels of MMOF-PANI, and thus effectively restrained the loss of active substance sulfur, resulting in high capacity and cycling stability of batteries. Compared with the reported batteries constructed with normal MOFs, the proposed battery with PANI-modified MMOF shows a satisfactory electrochemical performance (Table S2 in the ESM). Additionally, the IR spectra show that there is no obvious change in the IR spectra of electrodes before and after cycles (Fig. S19 in the ESM), indicating that no transformation of matter occurs on the electrode materials before and after charge–discharge processes.
It was reported that the porosity of materials usually leads to good adsorption for polysulfides [41]. The hierarchical porous structure provides a microreactor for LiPSs conversion. For MMOF-PANI, the adsorption isotherm of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) confirmed its microporous and mesoporous (Fig. S2 in the ESM), which will contribute to the high adsorption affinity for guest polysulfides. What's more, the open metal sites (OMSs) render the polarity of the channel surface and provide abundant active centers for the adsorption of sulfur and polysulfides [42]. It can be seen from the TGA that the weight loss of sulfur lasts up to \(300\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), and it shows an obvious change in the slope of the weight loss around \(300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) [43], indicating the existence of a strong interaction between the framework and sulfur, which may be the coordination of sulfur and Ni site confirmed by the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) characterization. To verify the interaction between MMOF-PANI and sulfur, the XPS characterization of MMOF-PANI and S@MMOF-PANI were implemented. For S@MMOF-PANI, the characteristic Ni 2p peak shifts to a higher energy region for \(0.5\ \mathrm{\eV}\) (Fig. 4(a)), indicating the significant strong interaction between Ni sites and sulfur. The N 1s peaks at 400.0 and \(398.3\;\mathrm{eV}\) can be assigned to the secondary amine of PANI and the tertiary amine of \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{NDISA}\) ligand (Fig. 4(b)). After sulfur loading, the emerging characteristic peak at \(401.6\;\mathrm{\eV}\) is attributed to the
quaternary ammonium, which may be obtained from tertiary amine by electron loss, indicating the formation of strong interaction between MMOF and sulfur. From another perspective, an obvious chemical shift of \(1.8\,\mathrm{eV}\) for S 2p spectra in S@MMOF-PANI could be observed compared with pristine sulfur [28], and a relatively small shift of about 0.6 to \(164.6\;\mathrm{\eV}\) of S \(2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}\) and \(163.4~\mathrm{eV}\) of S \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) were observed (Fig. 4(c)), indicating the two types of strong interaction between S and MMOF-PANI. The S 2p signals at high binding energy (168.1 and \(169.3\;\mathrm{\eV}\)) can be assigned to the S(IV) oxidation state. To further illustrate the effect of MMOF-PANI on polysulfides, the activated MMOF-PANI powder was soaked in a \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) DOL/DME solution, and it is obvious that the MMOF-PANI can absolutely adsorb \(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}\) (Fig. 4(d)). From the results we have obtained, it can be concluded that the MMOF-PANI with abundant OMSs can afford a potent binding effect of LiPSs and improve the cyclic stability and the rate performance of Li-S batteries. In a word, the hierarchical pores, the OMSs and the enhanced conductivity of MMOF-PANI make it a promising candidate as an excellent cathode material for Li-S batteries.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we incorporated PANI into the MMOF to enhance the discharge capacity and the cycling stability of the proposed Li-S batteries, as the stability and the conductivity of the MMOF cathode was improved simultaneously. The two-pronged strategy can effectively improve the conductivity and the stability of MMOF cathode simultaneously, which are very important for excellent Li-S battery performance. The MMOF-PANI was obtained through a solvothermal reaction in the presence of dissolved PANI. After activation, the effective cathode host material MMOF-PANI manifested an efficient restraint of polysulfides to improve the specific capacity and cycling stability of targeted batteries. Profit from the improved skeleton stability and intrinsic conductivity of MMOF-PANI and the superrich OMSs, the batteries showed prominent initial specific capacity with \(777.7\;\mathrm{mAh}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(2.0~\mathrm{C}\) and a remaining specific capacity of \(300.0\ \mathrm{\mAh{\cdot}g^{-1}}\) after 1,000 cycles. Meanwhile, it showed an excellent rate capability as the MMOF with hierarchical pores showed strong binding of soluble polysulfides. This work presents a wide range of possibilities for further development of MOF-based cathode materials for Li-S batteries.
